gs. `.` doesn't match newline. So JSON must be on one line! Ensure json.dumps produces single-line (default separators produce no newlines). Good \u2014 keep compact.\n\nAlso `.+?` non-greedy up to `}` followed by `\\s*;` \u2014 nested braces inside JSON: non-greedy will stop at first `}` that is followed by optional whitespace and `;`. Our JSON ends with `}};`? The module object contains nested objects (items with dicts). The regex `({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 it scans for `{`, then lazily expands until a `}` immediately followed by `;`. Nested closing braces are followed by `,` or `]` etc., not `;`, so expansion continues to the final `}` before `;`. But wait \u2014 lazy matching: it tries shortest match such that the remainder matches `\\s*;`. At each position of `}`, regex checks if next chars match `\\s*;`. Inside JSON, `}` followed by `,` fails; eventually last `}` followed by `;` succeeds. But what if a nested `}` is followed by whitespace then `;`? Only if JSON formatting puts `;` right after a nested close \u2014 not in our generated JSON. Good.\n\nSame for `player\\.playlist\\s*=\\s*(\\[.+?\\])\\s*;` \u2014 array must end with `];`. And `window\\.lesson\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;`.\n\nImportant: the lesson regexes are tried in order `(window\\.lesson..., player\\.lesson...)`; `_search_regex` iterates patterns. For the player.lesson test, ensure 'window.lesson' absent.\n\nTitle fallback regexes: `>Lecture\\s*:\\s*(?P<value>[^<]+)` \u2014 e.g., `<h1>Lecture : Foo</h1>` \u2014 wait, needs `>` before 'Lecture': `<h1>Lecture : Foo` \u2192 the char before 'Lecture' is '>' from `<h1>`. Match: `>Lecture\\s*:\\s*` then `[^<]+` captures 'Foo' (up to `<`). Value may include trailing spaces? `[^<]+` greedy \u2192 'Foo' if followed by '<'. If text is 'Lecture : Foo</h1>', captures 'Foo'. Good. Note `_search_regex` with group='value' returns that group. Trailing whitespace? 'Foo' clean.\n\nSecond alt: `lessonName\\s*=\\s*([\"\\'])(?P<value>(?:(?!\\1).)+)\\1` \u2014 e.g., `var lessonName = \"Backup Title\";` \u2192 value 'Backup Title'. Careful: the `;` after quote fine.\n\nNow single-video URL: needs chapter_id & lesson_id groups, no course_id. Per guessed regex shape `(?P<chapter_id>[^/?#]+)/lesson/(?P<lesson_id>[^/?#]+)` under linuxacademy.com domain. E.g., `https://linuxacademy.com/chapter-59/lesson/lecture-2`. item_id = 'chapter-59-lecture-2'.\n\nCourse URL: needs course_id group. Under guessed shape: multiple segments then course_id then `/lesson`. E.g., `https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/5248/lesson/2` \u2014 hmm which part is course_id? Unclear. Get the regex first!\n\nAlright, let me also double-check `try_get` semantics: `try_get(item, lambda x: x['type'][key], compat_str)` \u2014 returns None on error/None. Then `(None or '')` \u2192 ''. Good.\n\n`unified_timestamp(item.get('date')) or unified_timestamp(item.get('created_on'))` \u2014 for entry1 date valid; entry2 has created_on only.\n\nNow assertions for course test:\n\n```python\nresult = ie._real_extract(COURSE_URL)\nassert result['_type'] == 'playlist'\nassert result['id'] == COURSE_ID\nassert result['title'] == 'Course Title'\nassert result['description'] == 'Course MD'\nassert result['duration'] == 5400\nentries = result['entries']\nassert len(entries) == 2\ne1, e2 = entries\nassert e1['_type'] == 'url_transparent'\nassert e1['url'] == 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/intro'  # urljoin with base\nassert e1['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()\nassert e1['title'] == 'Intro Lesson'\nassert e1['description'] == 'MD desc'\nassert e1['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-06-25T10:30:00Z')\nassert e1['duration'] == 5400\nassert e1['chapter'] == 'Chapter One'\nassert e1['chapter_id'] == 'mod-1'\nassert e1['chapter_number'] == 1\ne2 checks: chapter 'Chapter Two', chapter_id 'mod-2', chapter_number 2, title 'Fallback Title', description 'Fallback Desc', timestamp == unified_timestamp('2019-06-26 08:00:00'), duration 2700, url full absolute.\n```\n\nWait: entry2 descri