\nNote: in newer Python (3.10+?), `urllib.parse.urljoin` with None url... doesn't matter due to wrapper.\n\nAlso note: youtube_dl.utils.urljoin checks `isinstance(path, compat_str)`. None \u2192 returns None. \n\nNow timestamps: `unified_timestamp('2019-06-25T10:30:00Z')`. unified_timestamp implementation: tries several patterns; ISO with Z handled? It does `date_from_str`? Let me recall:\n\n```python\ndef unified_timestamp(date_str, day_first=True):\n    if not isinstance(date_str, compat_str):\n        return None\n    date_str = re.sub(r'\\s+', ' ', date_str.strip())\n    date_str = re.sub(r'(?i)[,\\|]', ' ', date_str)\n    pm = re.search(r'(?i)(A|P)\\s*\\.?\\s*M\\s*$', date_str)\n    ...\n    # FIXME: This isn't quite correct\n    text = date_str.replace(',', ' ')\n    ...\n    m = re.search(r'(?i)(?<![0-9])(1[0-2]|0?[1-9])[/\\-\\.](0?[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])[/\\-\\.]((?:19|20)\\d{2})', text)\n    ...\n    for regex in (...):\n        m = re.match(regex, text)\n        ...\n```\n\nThere's a specific ISO handling:\n\n```python\n    m = re.search(\n        r'(?i)(?P<year>\\d{4})-(?P<month>\\d{1,2})-(?P<day>\\d{1,2})(?:[ T](?P<hour>\\d{1,2}):(?P<minute>\\d{1,2}):(?P<second>\\d{1,2})(?:\\.(?P<microsecond>\\d+))?)?(?:[\\.\\, ]?\\s*(?P<tz>Z|[+-]\\d{2}(?::?\\d{2})?))?$',\n        date_str)\n    if m:\n        ...\n```\n\nYes, unified_timestamp handles 'YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ'. Returns epoch int. Good.\n\n'2019-06-26 08:00:00' without tz \u2192 treated as local time? It builds datetime with timezone from tz or None \u2192 `calendar.timegm(dt.timetuple())` if tz else `time.mktime(dt.timetuple())`. Either way returns an int. For assertion simplicity, I'll assert `isinstance(ts, int)` or compute expected via unified_timestamp itself in the test (import from youtube_dl.utils). Using the same function in the test to compute expected value is acceptable and robust.\n\nDuration: parse_duration('1:30:00') \u2192 5400. parse_duration('45m') \u2192 2700. Good.\n\nPlaylist result asse