ncl. attributes.\n2. `get_info('LinuxAcademyIE._VALID_URL')`\n3. `get_info('LinuxAcademyIE._real_extract')`\n4. `get_info('InfoExtractor._sort_formats')`\n5. `get_info('InfoExtractor._download_webpage')`\n6. `get_info('InfoExtractor._extract_m3u8_formats')`\n\nAll independent \u2192 batch them.\n\n**Single video page**:\n\nWebpage:\n\n```html\n<script>player.playlist = [{\"file\":\"https://cdn.example.com/video.m3u8\"}];</script>\n<script>window.lesson = {\"lesson_name\":\"My Lecture\",\"md_desc\":\"Lesson MD\",\"date\":\"2019-06-25T10:30:00Z\",\"duration\":\"10:00\"};</script>\n```\n\nResult: info dict with id = 'chapter-lesson' composite, formats from mock, title/description/timestamp/duration from lesson.\n\nAssertions:\n- info['id'] == expected item_id\n- captured m3u8_url == 'https://cdn.example.com/video.m3u8'\n- captured ext 'mp4', entry_protocol 'm3u8_native', m3u8_id 'hls'\n- info['title'] == 'My Lecture'\n- info['description'] == 'Lesson MD'\n- info['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp(...)\n- info['duration'] == 600\n\n**Second single-video variant**: lesson JSON absent \u2192 default '{}' \u2192 falsy? Wait: `lesson = self._parse_json('{}', ...)` \u2192 {} \u2192 `if lesson:` False \u2192 skip update \u2192 then title fallback via `_search_regex` with `>Lecture\\s*:\\s*([^<]+)` or `lessonName\\s*=\\s*([\"\\'])(...)\\1`.\n\nWebpage:\n\n```html\n<div>Lecture : Intro to Things</div>\n<script>player.playlist = [{\"file\":\"https://cdn.example.com/v2.m3u8\"}];</script>\n```\n\nThen info['title'] == 'Intro to Things'. Covers lines 238-243.\n\nAlso cover the second title regex alternative? The `_search_regex` tries patterns in order; first match wins. To cover the `lessonName` branch, make a page where '>Lecture :' doesn't appear but `lessonName=\"Backup Name\"` does. Could be a third test or combine: one test uses Lecture form, another uses lessonName form. Both hit lines 238-243 anyway. Maybe do two variants to cover both regex alternatives \u2014 good for branch coverage of the tuple iteration (though tuples aren't branches per se; coverage counts lines 239-243 once). Still, nice to have.\n\nAlso cover `player.lesson` regex alternative (line 229) vs `window.lesson` (line 228): one test uses window.lesson, another uses player.lesson. \n\nAlso: what if lesson JSON parse yields falsy because default='{}' and regex finds nothing \u2192 `_search_regex` with default='{}' returns '{}' \u2192 parse \u2192 {} \u2192 falsy \u2192 title fallback. Covered above.\n\nWhat about `fatal=False` in parse_json \u2014 if JSON invalid, returns {}? With fatal=False, parse errors return default? `_parse_json(json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True)`: on exception, if fatal raise; else return None? Let me recall:\n\n```python\n    def _parse_json(self, json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True):\n        js = json.loads(json_string) ...\n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\n    def _parse_json(self, json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True):\n        try:\n            return json.loads(json_string)\n        except ValueError as ve:\n            ...\n            if fatal:\n                raise ExtractorError(...)\n            else:\n                return None\n```\n\nHmm, might return None on failure. `if lesson:` handles None too. Not needed for coverage though.\n\nNow, the m3u8 formats mock: return e.g.\n\n```python\n[\n    {'format_id': 'hls-720', 'url': 'https://cdn.example.com/720.m3u8', 'ext': 'mp4', 'protocol': 'm3u8_native', 'height': 720, 'tbr': 2000},\n    {'format_id': 'hls-360', 'url': 'https://cdn.example.com/360.m3u8', 'ext': 'mp4', 'protocol': 'm3u8_native', 'height': 360, 'tbr': 800},\n]\n```\n\nIf real `_sort_formats` runs, it sorts by preference \u2014 typically best quality first \u2192 720 first. I could assert `info['formats'][0]['height'] == 720`? Depends on sort implementation details (quality field default?). Risky. Safer: assert set of format_ids and that sort was called (via sp