{\n        'url': 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/5248/lesson/2/module/103',\n        'only_matching': True,\n    }, {\n        'url': 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/1499/lesson/2/module/103/chapter/59',\n        'only_matching': True,\n    }]\n```\n\nNot sure. The regex groups: chapter_id, lesson_id, course_id. A plausible regex:\n\n```python\n_VALID_URL = r'https?://(?:www\\.)?linuxacademy\\.com/(?:cp/)?courses?/(?P<course_id>[^/]+)(?:/chapter/(?P<chapter_id>[^/]+))?/lesson/(?P<lesson_id>[^/#?]+)'\n```\n\nI can't rely on memory. MUST use get_info. If get_info returns the class source, great.\n\nLet me also prepare fallback: if get_info gives nothing useful, I could write tests that derive URLs dynamically? Hard without knowing regex. Alternatively, tests could bypass regex by... no, line 155-156 need the regex.\n\nOK, let me start with get_info calls. Batch:\n\n- get_info('LinuxAcademyIE')\n- get_info('LinuxAcademyIE._VALID_URL')\n- get_info('LinuxAcademyIE._real_extract')\n- get_info('InfoExtractor._sort_formats')\n- get_info('InfoExtractor._download_webpage')\n- get_info('InfoExtractor._extract_m3u8_formats')\n\nMaybe also get_info('InfoExtractor.__init__') and get_info('InfoExtractor.ie_key') and get_info('InfoExtractor._search_regex') and get_info('InfoExtractor._parse_json'). That's a lot; but they're independent. Let me include the most critical ones. I'll do a first batch with the class + VALID_URL + sort_formats + download_webpage + extract_m3u8_formats + init. Then a second batch if needed.\n\nAfter getting info, write the test file.\n\nDraft test file (assuming regex confirmed):\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\nfrom __future__ import unicode_literals\n\nimport json\nimport re\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy import LinuxAcademyIE\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import unified_timestamp\n\n\nCOURSE_URL = 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/<course_id>/lesson/<lesson_id>'  # adjust per regex\n...\n```\n\nLet me write helpers:\n\n```python\ndef _make_ie(monkeypatch, webpage, formats=None):\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE()\n    downloaded = {}\n\n    def fake_download_webpage(url_or_request, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        downloaded['url'] = url_or_request\n        downloaded['video_id'] = video_id\n        return webpage\n\n    m3u8_calls = []\n\n    def fake_extract_m3u8(m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, entry_protocol='m3u8', **kwargs):\n        m3u8_calls.append({'m3u8_url': m3u8_url, 'video_id': video_id, 'ext': ext,\n                           'entry_protocol': entry_protocol,\n                           'm3u8_id': kwargs.get('m3u8_id')})\n        return [dict(f) for f in (formats or DEFAULT_FORMATS)]\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake_download_webpage)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_extract_m3u8_formats', fake_extract_m3u8)\n\n    real_sort = LinuxAcademyIE._sort_formats\n    sort_calls = []\n\n    def spy_sort(fmts):\n        sort_calls.append(fmts)\n        real_sort(ie, fmts)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_sort_formats', spy_sort)\n    return ie, downloaded, m3u8_calls, sort_calls\n```\n\nHmm, `spy_sort(fmts)` \u2014 instance attribute, so signature (fmts). real_sort(ie, fmts) calls unbound. OK.\n\nBut careful: if `_sort_formats` needs downloader params... check get_info result.\n\nCourse webpage builder:\n\n```python\ndef build_course_webpage(items, title='Course Title', md_desc='Course MD', desc='<b>Course HTML</b>', duration='1:30:00'):\n    module = {'title': title, 'md_desc': md_desc, 'desc': desc, 'duration': duration, 'items': items}\n    return '<html><head><script>window.module = %s;</script></head><body></body></html>' % json.dumps(module)\n```\n\nCareful: regex `window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 non-greedy `{.+?}` with DOTALL? `_search_regex` compiles with flags param default 0; but the pattern in code has no re.S... wait the code: `r'window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;'` \u2014 no flags. `.` doesn't match newline. So JSON must be on one line! Ensure json.dumps produces single-line (default separators produce no newlines). Good \u2014 keep compact.\n\nAlso `.+?` non-greedy up to `}` followed by `\\s*;` \u2014 nested braces inside JSON: non-greedy will stop at first `}` that is followed by optional whitespace and `;`. Our JSON ends with `}};`? The module object contains nested objects (items with dicts). The regex `({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 it scans for `{`, then lazily expands until a `}` immediately followed by `;`. Nested closing braces are followed by `,` or `]` etc., not `;`, so expansion continues to the final `}` before `;`. But wait \u2014 lazy matching: it tries shortest match such that the remainder matches `\\s*;`. At each position of `}`, regex checks if next chars match `\\s*;`. Inside JSON, `}` followed by `,` fails; eventually last `}` followed by `;` succeeds. But what if a nested `}` is followed by whitespace then `;`? Only if JSON formatting puts `;` right after a nested close \u2014 not in our generated JSON. Good.\n\nSame for `player\\.playlist\\s*=\\s*(\\[.+?\\])\\s*;` \u2014 array must end with `];`. And `window\\.lesson\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;`.\n\nImportant: the lesson regexes are tried in order `(window\\.lesson..., player\\.lesson...)`; `_search_regex` iterates patterns. For the player.lesson test, ensure 'window.lesson' absent.\n\nTitle fallback regexes: `>Lecture\\s*:\\s*(?P<value>[^<]+)` \u2014 e.g., `<h1>Lecture : Foo</h1>` \u2014 wait, needs `>` before 'Lecture': `<h1>Lecture : Foo` \u2192 the char before 'Lecture' is '>' from `<h1>`. Match: `>Lecture\\s*:\\s*` then `[^<]+` captures 'Foo' (up to `<`). Value may include trailing spaces? `[^<]+` greedy \u2192 'Foo' if followed by '<'. If text is 'Lecture : Foo</h1>', captures 'Foo'. Good. Note `_search_regex` with group='value' returns that group. Trailing whitespace? 'Foo' clean.\n\nSecond alt: `lessonName\\s*=\\s*([\"\\'])(?P<value>(?:(?!\\1).)+)\\1` \u2014 e.g., `var lessonName = \"Backup Title\";` \u2192 value 'Backup Title'. Careful: the `;` after quote fine.\n\nNow single-video URL: needs chapter_id & lesson_id groups, no course_id. Per guessed regex shape `(?P<chapter_id>[^/?#]+)/lesson/(?P<lesson_id>[^/?#]+)` under linuxacademy.com domain. E.g., `https://linuxacademy.com/chapter-59/lesson/lecture-2`. item_id = 'chapter-59-lecture-2'.\n\nCourse URL: needs course_id group. Under guessed shape: multiple segments then course_id then `/lesson`. E.g., `https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/5248/lesson/2` \u2014 hmm which part is course_id? Unclear. Get the regex first!\n\nAlright, let me also double-check `try_get` semantics: `try_get(item, lambda x: x['type'][key], compat_str)` \u2014 returns None on error/None. Then `(None or '')` \u2192 ''. Good.\n\n`unified_timestamp(item.get('date')) or unified_timestamp(item.get('created_on'))` \u2014 for entry1 date valid; entry2 has created_on only.\n\nNow assertions for course test:\n\n```python\nresult = ie._real_extract(COURSE_URL)\nassert result['_type'] == 'playlist'\nassert result['id'] == COURSE_ID\nassert result['title'] == 'Course Title'\nassert result['description'] == 'Course MD'\nassert result['duration'] == 5400\nentries = result['entries']\nassert len(entries) == 2\ne1, e2 = entries\nassert e1['_type'] == 'url_transparent'\nassert e1['url'] == 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/intro'  # urljoin with base\nassert e1['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()\nassert e1['title'] == 'Intro Lesson'\nassert e1['description'] == 'MD desc'\nassert e1['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-06-25T10:30:00Z')\nassert e1['duration'] == 5400\nassert e1['chapter'] == 'Chapter One'\nassert e1['chapter_id'] == 'mod-1'\nassert e1['chapter_number'] == 1\ne2 checks: chapter 'Chapter Two', chapter_id 'mod-2', chapter_number 2, title 'Fallback Title', description 'Fallback Desc', timestamp == unified_timestamp('2019-06-26 08:00:00'), duration 2700, url full absolute.\n```\n\nWait: entry2 description: