a spy wrapper). Or assert `[f['height'] for f in formats] == sorted([...], reverse=True)`? Default sort: `quality` higher first; computed quality from height/tbr/resolution. I think asserting descending heights is likely correct but not guaranteed across versions. I'll assert formats contain both ids and that the spy recorded a call with the same formats list. Keep it robust.\n\nHmm, wait: does `_sort_formats` mutate in place? Yes, `formats.sort(key=...)`. The spy passes same list object; after real sort, list reordered. Assert both format_ids present.\n\nPotential issue: `_sort_formats` in some versions accesses `self._downloader.params` unguarded? Let me check via get_info('InfoExtractor._sort_formats'). If risky, set a dummy downloader? Setting a real YoutubeDL instance is heavy. Alternative: monkeypatch `ie._downloader`? It's None by default; code paths guard with `if self._downloader`. I'll verify via get_info.\n\nAnother consideration: `InfoExtractor.__init__` \u2014 check signature via get_info('InfoExtractor.__init__').\n\nAlso `ie_key()`: classmethod returning `cls._IE_KEY` or `cls.ie_name()`. For LinuxAcademyIE, `_IE_NAME = 'linuxacademy'`? ie_key returns cls._IE_KEY if set else ie_name(). Probably 'LinuxAcademy'. I'll assert `entry['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()` rather than hardcoding.\n\nNow about importing: `from youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy import LinuxAcademyIE`. The extractor module imports `.common` which imports lots \u2014 fine.\n\nInstantiation: `LinuxAcademyIE()` \u2014 InfoExtractor.__init__(self, downloader=None). OK.\n\nOne more subtlety: `_real_extract` is called directly by us; normally `extract(url)` wraps with suitable checks etc. Calling `_real_extract(url)` directly is fine and avoids network.\n\nBut wait \u2014 `self._download_webpage` mocked at instance level: assign `ie._download_webpage = fake` \u2014 instance attribute shadows bound method. Works.\n\nHowever, monkeypatch teardown: setting instance attributes persists on