me = \"Backup Title\";` \u2192 value 'Backup Title'. Careful: the `;` after quote fine.\n\nNow single-video URL: needs chapter_id & lesson_id groups, no course_id. Per guessed regex shape `(?P<chapter_id>[^/?#]+)/lesson/(?P<lesson_id>[^/?#]+)` under linuxacademy.com domain. E.g., `https://linuxacademy.com/chapter-59/lesson/lecture-2`. item_id = 'chapter-59-lecture-2'.\n\nCourse URL: needs course_id group. Under guessed shape: multiple segments then course_id then `/lesson`. E.g., `https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/5248/lesson/2` \u2014 hmm which part is course_id? Unclear. Get the regex first!\n\nAlright, let me also double-check `try_get` semantics: `try_get(item, lambda x: x['type'][key], compat_str)` \u2014 returns None on error/None. Then `(None or '')` \u2192 ''. Good.\n\n`unified_timestamp(item.get('date')) or unified_timestamp(item.get('created_on'))` \u2014 for entry1 date valid; entry2 has created_on only.\n\nNow assertions for course test:\n\n```python\nresult = ie._real_extract(COURSE_URL)\nassert result['_type'] == 'playlist'\nassert result['id'] == COURSE_ID\nassert result['title'] == 'Course Title'\nassert result['description'] == 'Course MD'\nassert result['duration'] == 5400\nentries = result['entries']\nassert len(entries) == 2\ne1, e2 = entries\nassert e1['_type'] == 'url_transparent'\nassert e1['url'] == 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/intro'  # urljoin with base\nassert e1['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()\nassert e1['title'] == 'Intro Lesson'\nassert e1['description'] == 'MD desc'\nassert e1['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-06-25T10:30:00Z')\nassert e1['duration'] == 5400\nassert e1['chapter'] == 'Chapter One'\nassert e1['chapter_id'] == 'mod-1'\nassert e1['chapter_number'] == 1\ne2 checks: chapter 'Chapter Two', chapter_id 'mod-2', chapter_number 2, title 'Fallback Title', description 'Fallback Desc', timestamp == unified_timestamp('2019-06-26 08:00:00'), duration 2700, url full absolute.\n```\n\nWait: entry2 description: `item.get('md_desc') or clean_html(item.get('description')) or clean_html(item.get('text'))` \u2192 md_desc None \u2192 clean_html('<i>Fallback Desc</i>') \u2192 'Fallback Desc'. Good. This covers clean_html branch.\n\nHmm, `unified_timestamp('2019-06-26 08:00:00')` \u2014 no tz \u2192 uses mktime (local). Test computes expected with same function \u2192 consistent. But is '2019-06-26 08:00:00' parsed? The ISO regex allows space separator: `[ T]`. Yes. Good.\n\nAlso note: for entry1, `timestamp` uses `date`; also covers the `or` short-circuit (created_on not evaluated). For entry2, date missing \u2192 unified_timestamp(None) \u2192 None \u2192 falls to created_on. Covers both sides of `or`.\n\nurljoin base: course URL e.g. 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/5248/lesson/2' + relative '/lesson/intro' \u2192 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/intro'. Absolute URL passthrough: 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/full' stays. Good.\n\nNon-dict item: include `\"just-a-string\"` in items. Also maybe include `42`? One is enough.\n\nDict without 'type': `{}` \u2192 type_fields ('','') \u2192 not section, not lesson \u2192 skip. Covers try_get returning None path.\n\nItem with type dict but neither name nor slug keys: `{'type': {}}` \u2192 same skip. Optional.\n\nLesson without url: `{'type': {'name': 'lesson'}, 'title': 'No URL'}` \u2192 urljoin(url, None) \u2192 None \u2192 skip. Covers 188-189.\n\nWait \u2014 but hold on: does `urljoin` wrapper exist in this youtube_dl version? The snippet imports `urljoin` from youtube_dl.utils. The wrapper:\n\n```python\ndef urljoin(base, path):\n    if isinstance(path, bytes):\n        path = path.decode('utf-8')\n    if not isinstance(path, compat_str) or not path:\n        return None\n    return compat_urllib_parse_urljoin(base, path)\n```\n\nI believe modern youtube_dl has this wrapper (added ~2018). If instead it's plain urllib urljoin aliased, `urljoin(base, None)` \u2192 in Py3, `urljoin('http://a/', None)`: implementation `if not url: return base`? Let me check CPython:\n\n```python\ndef urljoin(base, url, allow_fragments=True):\n    if not base:\n        return url\n    if not url:\n        return base\n    bscheme, bnetloc, bpath, bparams, bquery, bfragment = urlparse(base, '', allow_fragments)\n    ...\n```\n\nCPython's urljoin: `if not url: return base`. So None \u2192 returns base \u2192 lesson_url = course URL (truthy!) \u2192 entry appended with url == course URL. That changes behavior! Which one does youtube_dl.utils expose?\n\nChecking youtube_dl/utils.py (master):\n\n```python\ndef urljoin(base, path):\n    if isinstance(path, bytes):\n        path = path.decode('utf-8')\n    if not isinstance(path, compat_str) or not path:\n        return None\n    return compat_urllib_parse_urljoin(base, path)\n```\n\nYes, this wrapper exists in youtube_dl (and yt-dlp inherited it). It was added in 2017-ish commit \"utils.urljoin\". Given the snippet imports urljoin from youtube_dl.utils and uses `if not lesson_url: continue`, the wrapper semantics (None for falsy path) is clearly intended. I'll verify via get_info('youtube_dl.utils.urljoin')? The tool takes names like 'f' \u2014 maybe module-qualified works. I'll try get_info('urljoin') too. Eh \u2014 I can include it in the batch; cheap.\n\nTo be safe in the test, I can assert the no-url lesson is skipped (len(entries)==2). If urljoin were the raw urllib one, the entry would be included and test fails \u2014 but then the code's `if not lesson_url` branch would be dead anyway. The presence of that branch strongly implies wrapper semantics. Proceed.\n\nNow single-video test 1 (window.lesson present):\n\nwebpage:\n\n```html\n<html><body>\n<script>player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://cdn.example.com/hls/master.m3u8\"}, {\"notfile\": true}];</script>\n<script>window.lesson = {\"lesson_name\": \"My Lecture\", \"md_desc\": \"Lesson MD\", \"date\": \"2019-06-25T10:30:00Z\", \"duration\": \"10:00\"};</script>\n</body></html>\n```\n\