ion `if not url: return base`? Let me check CPython:\n\n```python\ndef urljoin(base, url, allow_fragments=True):\n    if not base:\n        return url\n    if not url:\n        return base\n    bscheme, bnetloc, bpath, bparams, bquery, bfragment = urlparse(base, '', allow_fragments)\n    ...\n```\n\nCPython's urljoin: `if not url: return base`. So None \u2192 returns base \u2192 lesson_url = course URL (truthy!) \u2192 entry appended with url == course URL. That changes behavior! Which one does youtube_dl.utils expose?\n\nChecking youtube_dl/utils.py (master):\n\n```python\ndef urljoin(base, path):\n    if isinstance(path, bytes):\n        path = path.decode('utf-8')\n    if not isinstance(path, compat_str) or not path:\n        return None\n    return compat_urllib_parse_urljoin(base, path)\n```\n\nYes, this wrapper exists in youtube_dl (and yt-dlp inherited it). It was added in 2017-ish commit \"utils.urljoin\". Given the snippet imports urljoin from youtube_dl.utils and uses `if not lesson_url: continue`, the wrapper semantics (None for falsy path) is clearly intended. I'll verify via get_info('youtube_dl.utils.urljoin')? The tool takes names like 'f' \u2014 maybe module-qualified works. I'll try get_info('urljoin') too. Eh \u2014 I can include it in the batch; cheap.\n\nTo be safe in the test, I can assert the no-url lesson is skipped (len(entries)==2). If urljoin were the raw urllib one, the entry would be included and test fails \u2014 but then the code's `if not lesson_url` branch would be dead anyway. The presence of that branch strongly implies wrapper semantics. Proceed.\n\nNow single-video test 1 (window.lesson present):\n\nwebpage:\n\n```html\n<html><body>\n<script>player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://cdn.example.com/hls/master.m3u8\"}, {\"notfile\": true}];</script>\n<script>window.lesson = {\"lesson_name\": \"My Lecture\", \"md_desc\": \"Lesson MD\", \"date\": \"2019-06-25T10:30:00Z\", \"duration\": \"10:00\"};</script>\n</body></html>\n```\n\nRegex