tractor that calls self._downloader.to_screen. Whatever \u2014 adding these no-op methods is harmless.\n\nWait, actually there's a subtlety: `InfoExtractor.report_warning` does:\n\n```python\n    def report_warning(self, msg, video_id=None):\n        ...\n        if self._downloader:\n            self._downloader.report_warning(...)\n        else:\n            self.to_screen(...)\n```\n\nIf `_downloader` set (our fake) it calls `self._downloader.report_warning(msg)` \u2014 fake has it. Fine. And `to_screen` on IE calls `self._downloader.to_screen`. Fine.\n\nAlso `_parse_json` on error with fatal=False calls `self.report_warning`? Possibly. Our fake handles.\n\nLet me still fetch set_downloader, _search_regex, _parse_json, ie_key to be precise.\n\nAlso \u2014 one more consideration for the course test: `_search_regex('window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;', webpage, 'module')` \u2014 real regex against our webpage. Our JSON must be single-line (no newlines inside since `.` doesn't match \\n without DOTALL). json.dumps default \u2192 single line. \u2713 But careful: the JSON contains `;`? No semicolons in JSON values \u2014 ensure description strings don't contain ';'. My strings don't. Also ensure no `} ;` sequences inside... The lazy match stops at first `}` followed by `\\s*;`. Inside JSON, `}` is always followed by `,` `]` `}` or end. The final `}` followed by `;`. \u2713\n\nBut wait \u2014 nested objects: e.g. `{\"type\": {\"name\": \"Section\"}}` \u2014 inner `}` followed by `}`. Fine.\n\nAlso the playlist regex `player\\.playlist\\s*=\\s*(\\[.+?\\])\\s*;` \u2014 `.+?` lazy until `]` followed by `;`. Our array: `[{\"file\": \"https://...m3u8\"}, {\"x\": 1}]` \u2014 inner `]`? none. Final `]` followed by `;`. \u2713 Ensure no ']' inside strings.\n\nLesson regex `window\\.lesson\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;` \u2014 same as module. \u2713\n\nTitle regex `>Lecture\\s*:\\s*(?P<value>[^<]+)` \u2014 our `<h1>Lecture : Backup Lecture Title</h1>`: the `>` before 'Lecture' is from