ll use the function call for timestamps and parse_duration for durations where the string is non-trivial, but also assert concrete ints for the simple colon forms (5400, 600, 2700) \u2014 those are certain.\n\nActually for '2019-06-26 08:00:00' (no tz) \u2014 unified_timestamp uses local timezone via mktime \u2192 machine-dependent value. So must compute expected via unified_timestamp in the test (same process, same tz) \u2192 consistent. \u2713\n\nNow, `parse_duration('45:00')` \u2192 2700 \u2713. '1:30:00' \u2192 5400 \u2713. '10:00' \u2192 600 \u2713.\n\nCourse module duration '1:30:00' \u2192 5400.\n\nNow let me also double-check `try_get` import path \u2014 code imports from youtube_dl.utils; fine.\n\n`compat_str` \u2014 fine.\n\nNow the FakeYDL: `_sort_formats` accesses `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')` \u2014 our fake has params={}. \u2713 Also `_formats_key` may call `determine_protocol(f)` \u2014 f has 'protocol' \u2192 fine.\n\nset_downloader \u2014 need to check if it touches downloader.cookiejar. Let me get_info it. Also ie_key/ie_name to assert ie_key value ('LinuxAcademy' presumably; _IE_NAME likely 'LinuxAcademy'). I'll assert `entry['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()` \u2014 no hardcode needed. But knowing ie_key exists as classmethod is enough.\n\nBatch 2 calls:\n- InfoExtractor.set_downloader\n- InfoExtractor._search_regex\n- InfoExtractor._parse_json\n- InfoExtractor.ie_key\n\nThen write final tests.\n\nTest module layout:\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\nfrom __future__ import unicode_literals\n\nimport json\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.linuxacademy import LinuxAcademyIE\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import parse_duration, unified_timestamp\n\nCOURSE_URL = 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/154'\nCOURSE_ID = '154'\n\nLESSON_URL = 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/courses/lesson/course/7971/lesson/2'\nLESSON_ITEM_ID = '7971-2'\n\nDEFAULT_FORMATS = [...]\n\n\nclass _FakeDownloader(object):\n    \"\"\"Stand-in for YoutubeDL: only ``params`` (and logging no-ops) are used.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {}\n\n    def to_screen(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        pass\n\n    def report_warning(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        pass\n\n\ndef _instrument(monkeypatch, webpage, formats=None):\n    ie = LinuxAcademyIE(_FakeDownloader())\n    calls = {'downloads': [], 'm3u8': [], 'sorts': []}\n\n    def fake_download_webpage(url_or_request, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        calls['downloads'].append({'url': url_or_request, 'video_id': video_id})\n        return webpage\n\n    def fake_extract_m3u8_formats(m3u8_url, video_id, ext=None, entry_protocol='m3u8', **kwargs):\n        calls['m3u8'].append({\n            'm3u8_url': m3u8_url,\n            'video_id': video_id,\n            'ext': ext,\n            'entry_protocol': entry_protocol,\n            'm3u8_id': kwargs.get('m3u8_id'),\n        })\n        return [dict(f) for f in (formats if formats is not None else DEFAULT_FORMATS)]\n\n    real_sort = LinuxAcademyIE._sort_formats\n\n    def spy_sort(fmts, *args, **kwargs):\n        calls['sorts'].append(fmts)\n        return real_sort(ie, fmts, *args, **kwargs)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake_download_webpage)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_extract_m3u8_formats', fake_extract_m3u8_formats)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_sort_formats', spy_sort)\n    return ie, calls\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_sort_formats', spy_sort)`: monkeypatch on instance attribute that doesn't exist on instance (it's on class) \u2014 monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value) works: it sets instance attr and on undo deletes it (since it didn't exist on the instance? Actually monkeypatch records `setattr` and on undo restores by `delattr` if it wasn't in obj's __dict__ before \u2014 yes, monkeypatch handles that: it checks if attribute existed via getattr on obj; for missing attrs it uses `delattr` on undo... hmm, actually monkeypatch.setattr with an object that doesn't have the attribute raises AttributeError unless raising=False. Wait: `monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value)` \u2014 if obj doesn't have `name`, it raises AttributeError unless `raising=False`. Does `ie` \"have\" `_sort_formats`? getattr(ie, '_sort_formats') finds the bound method via class \u2192 yes it exists (via class). monkeypatch.setattr checks `hasattr`? It does `oldval = getattr(obj, name, ...)`? Let me recall pytest monkeypatch implementation:\n\n```python\n    def setattr(self, target, name, value=notset, raising=True):\n        ...\n        oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\n        if raising and oldval is notset:\n            raise AttributeError(...)\n        ...\n        self._setattr.append((target, name, oldval))\n        setattr(target, name, value)\n```\n\nAnd undo:\n\n```python\n    def undo(self):\n        for obj, name, value in reversed(self._setattr):\n            if value is not notset:\n                setattr(obj, name, value)\n            else:\n                delattr(obj, name)\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 `getattr(ie, '_sort_formats')` returns bound method (found on class) \u2192 oldval = bound method \u2192 undo does `setattr(ie, '_sort_formats', <bound method>)` \u2192 sets an INSTANCE attribute pointing to the bound method. That leaves instance attr after undo! Wait no \u2014 undo sets it back as instance attribute, which shadows class method but with identical behavior. Hmm, that's a subtle residue: after monkeypatch.undo, `ie._sort_formats` is an instance attr = bound method \u2014 functionally identical. But since each test creates a fresh `ie`, no cross-test pollution at all. The `ie` object is garbage after test. Fine.\n\nActually wait, there's a subtlety: `delattr(ie, '_sort_formats')` would fail? No \u2014 undo uses oldval which was the bound method (notset only if getattr raised). getattr succeeded \u2192 oldval = bound method \u2192 setattr back. OK. No pollution concern since ie is local.\n\nAlternatively use `monkeypatch.setattr(LinuxAcademyIE, '_sort_formats', ...)` at class level with proper self param \u2014 but instance-level is cleaner per-test.\n\nHmm, one more: `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake)` \u2014 same pattern. Fine.\n\nNow the course webpage builder and test:\n\n```python\ndef _course_webpage():\n    module = {\n        'title': 'AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner',\n        'md_desc': '',\n        'desc': '<b>Course HTML description</b>',\n        'duration': '1:30:00',\n        'items': [\n            'not-a-dict',\n            {},\n            {\n                'type': {'name': 'Section', 'slug': 'section'},\n                'course_name': 'Chapter One',\n                'course_module': 'module-1',\n            },\n            {'type': {'name': 'Quiz'}},\n            {'type': {'name': 'lesson'}, 'title': 'Orphan lesson (no url)'},\n            {\n                'type': {'name': 'lesson', 'slug': 'LESSON'},\n                'url': '/lesson/intro',\n                'title': 'Intro Lesson',\n                'md_desc': 'Intro markdown description',\n                'date': '2019-06-25T10:30:00Z',\n                'duration': '1:30:00',\n            },\n            {\n                'type': {'name': 'SECTION'},\n                'course_name': 'Chapter Two',\n                'course_module': 'module-2',\n            },\n            {\n                'type': {'slug': 'lesson'},\n                'url': 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/fallback',\n                'lesson_name': 'Fallback Lesson',\n                'description': '<i>Fallback description</i>',\n                'created_on': '2019-06-26 08:00:00',\n                'duration': '45:00',\n            },\n            {\n                'type': {'slug': 'lesson'},\n                'url': '/lesson/text-only',\n                'title': 'Text Only Lesson',\n                'text': '<b>Text description</b>',\n            },\n        ],\n    }\n    return (\n        '<html><head><script>window.module = ' + json.dumps(module) + ';</script>'\n        '</head><body>course page</body></html>'\n    )\n```\n\nWait \u2014 careful with the module regex: `window\\.module\\s*=\\s*({.+?})\\s*;`. Our script: `window.module = {...};`. \u2713 single line.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 the JSON contains the string '2019-06-25T10:30:00Z' etc. No braces/semicolons issues. The `desc` value '<b>Course HTML description</b>' \u2014 no braces. \u2713\n\nTest:\n\n```python\ndef test_course_playlist(monkeypatch):\n    ie, calls = _instrument(monkeypatch, _course_webpage())\n\n    result = ie._real_extract(COURSE_URL)\n\n    # webpage was requested with the course id as video_id\n    assert calls['downloads'] == [{'url': COURSE_URL, 'video_id': COURSE_ID}]\n    # single-video path must not run\n    assert calls['m3u8'] == []\n    assert calls['sorts'] == []\n\n    assert result['_type'] == 'playlist'\n    assert result['id'] == COURSE_ID\n    assert result['title'] == 'AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner'\n    # md_desc is empty -> falls back to clean_html(desc)\n    assert result['description'] == 'Course HTML description'\n    assert result['duration'] == 5400\n\n    entries = result['entries']\n    assert len(entries) == 3\n\n    first, second, third = entries\n    for entry in entries:\n        assert entry['_type'] == 'url_transparent'\n        assert entry['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()\n\n    assert first['url'] == 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/intro'\n    assert first['title'] == 'Intro Lesson'\n    assert first['description'] == 'Intro markdown description'\n    assert first['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-06-25T10:30:00Z')\n    assert first['duration'] == 5400\n    assert first['chapter'] == 'Chapter One'\n    assert first['chapter_id'] == 'module-1'\n    assert first['chapter_number'] == 1\n\n    assert second['url'] == 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/fallback'\n    assert second['title'] == 'Fallback Lesson'\n    assert second['description'] == 'Fallback description'\n    assert second['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-06-26 08:00:00')\n    assert second['duration'] == 2700\n    assert second['chapter'] == 'Chapter Two'\n    assert second['chapter_id'] == 'module-2'\n    assert second['chapter_number'] == 2\n\n    assert third['url'] == 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/text-only'\n    assert third['title'] == 'Text Only Lesson'\n    assert third['description'] == 'Text description'\n    assert third['timestamp'] is None\n    assert third['duration'] is None\n    assert third['chapter'] == 'Chapter Two'\n    assert third['chapter_id'] == 'module-2'\n    assert third['chapter_number'] == 2\n```\n\nWait \u2014 third entry url: base 'https://linuxacademy.com/cp/modules/view/id/154' + '/lesson/text-only' \u2192 'https://linuxacademy.com/lesson/text-only'. \u2713\n\nHmm \u2014 one thing: `unified_timestamp('2019-06-26 08:00:00')` \u2014 does unified_timestamp accept space-separated ISO? The ISO regex in youtube_dl utils: \n\n```python\n    m = re.search(\n        r'(?i)(?P<year>\\d{4})-(?P<month>\\d{1,2})-(?P<day>\\d{1,2})(?:[ T](?P<hour>\\d{1,2}):(?P<minute>\\d{1,2}):(?P<second>\\d{1,2})(?:\\.(?P<microsecond>\\d+))?)?(?:[\\.\\, ]?\\s*(?P<tz>Z|[+-]\\d{2}(?::?\\d{2})?))?$',\n        date_str)\n```\n\nHmm, I recall something like that; '2019-06-26 08:00:00' should match with hour/minute/second and no tz. Then:\n\n```python\n        if timezone: ...\n        else: timestamp = calendar.timegm(...) or time.mktime?\n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\n            timestamp = calendar.timegm(dt.timetuple())\n```\n\nHmm, if no tz info, youtube_dl treats it as UTC? Let me recall unified_timestamp:\n\n```python\ndef unified_timestamp(date_str, day_first=True):\n    ...\n    if m:\n        timezone = ...\n        if not timezone:\n            ...?\n```\n\nHonestly, whether it's UTC or local doesn't matter \u2014 the test computes expected with the same function in the same process, so it matches regardless. \u2713 (Both the extractor and the test call the same pure function with the same string.)\n\nBut one caveat: `unified_timestamp` might return None for some strings if parsing fails. '2019-06-26 08:00:00' \u2014 I'm fairly confident it parses (ISO-ish with space). To be extra safe, I could assert `second['timestamp'] == unified_timestamp('2019-06-26 08:00:00')` \u2014 if that returns None, then entry timestamp is None and assertion `None == None` passes trivially \u2014 weakening the test but not failing. Hmm, that would silently weaken. Let me add a sanity assert: `assert unified_timestamp('2019-06-26 08:00:00') is not None` \u2014 guards against the degenerate case. Similarly for the Z one, hardcode 1561458600? I computed 1561458600; let me double-check with another method: 2019-06-25T10:30:00Z. Known reference: 2019-06-25 00:00:00 UTC = 1561411200? Let me recompute June 25 00:00: 1559347200 (June 1) + 24*86400 = 1559347200+2073600 = 1561420800. Hmm I got 1561420800 before, now 1561411200? Recompute: 1559347200 + 2073600 = 1561420800. Yes 1561420800. Check: 1561420800 / 86400 = 18072.0 days exactly. 18072 days from epoch \u2192 1970 + 49.48 years \u2248 mid-2019 \u2713. And 2019-01-01 = 1546300800 \u2192 /86400 = 17901 days. 17901 + 151 = 18052 (June 1). Hmm! 17901+151 = 18052, not 18072. Let me recompute: 1546300800/86400: 1546300800 / 86400 = 17901.0? 86400*17901 = 86400*18000 - 86400*99 = 1555200000 - 8553600 = 1546646400. That's not 1546300800. So 1546300800/86400 = 17896.9...? Let me just do it cleanly: 86400 * 17900 = 1546560000. 1546300800 is less: 1546560000 - 1546300800 = 259200 = 3 days. So 1546300800 = 86400 * (17900 - 3) = 86400*17897. So 2019-01-01 = day 17897. Check known: 2018-01-01 = 1514764800 \u2192 /86400 = 17532. 2018 has 365 days \u2192 2019-01-01 = 17532+365 = 17897 \u2713. Good.\n\nJune 1 2019 = 17897 + 151 = 18048 days \u2192 18048*86400 = 1559347200 \u2713 (matches earlier). June 25 = 18048+24 = 18072 days \u2192 18072*86400 = 1561420800 \u2713. +37800s (10:30) = 1561458600. So unified_timestamp('2019-06-25T10:30:00Z') == 1561458600. I'll hardcode this and also keep the function-based check? Hardcoding is a stronger assertion. But if my arithmetic is off by an hour... risk. Use both: `assert first['timestamp'] == 1561458600`? If wrong, test fails wrongly. Safer: `expected = unified_timestamp('2019-06-25T10:30:00Z'); assert expected is not None; assert first['timestamp'] == expected`. This is robust and still meaningful (verifies plumbing; the function itself is youtube_dl's own, tested elsewhere). I'll do that pattern for all timestamps, with the not-None sanity check.\n\nDurations: hardcode 5400/2700/600 \u2014 parse_duration on colon forms is unambiguous. \u2713\n\nNow single-video tests.\n\nTest 2: window.lesson present:\n\n```python\ndef test_lesson_with_window_lesson_metadata(monkeypatch):\n    webpage = (\n        '<html><body>'\n        '<script>player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://cdn.example.com/hls/master.m3u8\"}];</script>'\n        '<script>window.lesson = ' + json.dumps({\n            'lesson_name': 'What Is Data Science',\n            'md_desc': 'Lesson markdown description',\n            'date': '2020-12-08T21:18:27Z',\n            'duration': '10:00',\n        }) + ';</script>'\n        '</body></html>'\n    )\n    ie, calls = _instrument(monkeypatch, webpage)\n\n    info = ie._real_extract(LESSON_URL)\n\n    assert calls['downloads'] == [{'url': LESSON_URL, 'video_id': LESSON_ITEM_ID}]\n\n    # m3u8 playlist handling\n    assert len(calls['m3u8']) == 1\n    m3u8 = calls['m3u8'][0]\n    assert m3u8['m3u8_url'] == 'https://cdn.example.com/hls/master.m3u8'\n    assert m3u8['video_id'] == LESSON_ITEM_ID\n    assert m3u8['ext'] == 'mp4'\n    assert m3u8['entry_protocol'] == 'm3u8_native'\n    assert m3u8['m3u8_id'] == 'hls'\n\n    # formats were sorted (worst first, best last)\n    assert len(calls['sorts']) == 1\n    assert calls['sorts'][0] is info['formats']\n    assert {f['format_id'] for f in info['formats']} == {'hls-720', 'hls-360'}\n    tbrs = [f['tbr'] for f in info['formats']]\n    assert tbrs == sorted(tbrs)\n\n    assert info['id'] == LESSON_ITEM_ID\n    assert info['title'] == 'What Is Data Science'\n    assert info['description'] == 'Lesson markdown description'\n    expected_ts = unified_timestamp('2020-12-08T21:18:27Z')\n    assert expected_ts is not None\n    assert info['timestamp'] == expected_ts\n    assert info['duration'] == 600\n```\n\nWait \u2014 the playlist regex: `player\\.playlist\\s*=\\s*(\\[.+?\\])\\s*;` \u2014 our string: `player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://cdn.example.com/hls/master.m3u8\"}];` \u2014 lazy `\\[.+?\\]` stops at first `]` followed by `;`. The only `]` is the final one. \u2713 But careful: the URL contains no ']'. \u2713\n\nAlso `window.lesson` JSON single line \u2713. Note: the lesson regex tried first is `window\\.lesson...` \u2192 matches. \u2713\n\nTimestamp '2020-12-08T21:18:27Z' \u2014 from the real _TESTS (timestamp 1607387907 for 'What Is Data Science'). Nice touch but I'll compute via function anyway.\n\nTest 3: player.lesson + desc fallback + Lecture title fallback:\n\n```python\ndef test_lesson_player_lesson_and_lecture_title_fallback(monkeypatch):\n    webpage = (\n        '<html><body><h1>Lecture : Backup Lecture Title</h1>'\n        '<script>player.playlist = [{\"file\": \"https://cdn.example.com/hls/two.m3u8\"}];</script>'\n        '<script>player.lesson = ' + json.dumps({\n            'lesson_name': '',\n            'desc': '<p>HTML lesson description</p>',\n            'created_on': '2019-07-01 05:06:07',\n            'duration': '45:00',\n        }) + ';</script>'\n        '</body></html>'\n    )\n    ie, calls = _instrument(monkeypatch, webpage)\n\n    info = ie._real_extract(LESSON_URL)\n\n    assert info['id'] == LESSON_ITEM_ID\n    # lesson dict is truthy -> info updated from it\n    assert info['description'] == 'HTML lesson description'\n    expected_ts = unified_timestamp('2019-07-01 05: