{f['format_id'] for f in info['formats']} == {'hls-720', 'hls-360'}\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 `set(info.keys()) == {'id', 'formats', 'title'}` \u2014 strict; if implementation adds nothing else, \u2713 per the code shown. The code: info = {'id','formats'}; lesson falsy \u2192 no update; title fallback adds 'title'. So keys exactly {id, formats, title}. \u2713 Strong assertion. But is it too brittle? It matches the given code exactly. Keep.\n\nWait \u2014 one concern in test 4: `_search_regex` with tuple patterns and default='{}': first pattern `window\\.lesson...` no match; second `player\\.lesson...` no match \u2192 returns default '{}'. Then `_parse_json('{}', item_id, fatal=False)` \u2192 {} \u2192 falsy. \u2713 Then title search: first pattern no match, second matches. \u2713\n\nAlso \u2014 does `_search_regex` log anything on default path that would touch downloader? Possibly `self.report_warning`? Only in the fatal=False-no-default path. With default, returns silently (I believe). FakeDownloader has no-op methods anyway. But wait \u2014 FakeDownloader lacks many attributes; if any code path calls `self._downloader.to_screen` \u2192 we have it. `self._downloader.params.get` \u2192 have. OK.\n\nHmm, also `_parse_json` with fatal=False on success \u2014 no logging. \u2713\n\nNow \u2014 one more possible gotcha: `InfoExtractor.__init__` \u2192 `set_downloader(downloader)`. Need to verify set_downloader doesn't require more from downloader. Let me get_info it. Also `ie_key`/`ie_name` \u2014 verify classmethod exists and what it returns (for the assertion `entry['ie_key'] == LinuxAcademyIE.ie_key()` \u2014 tautological but fine; maybe also assert it's a non-empty string).\n\nAlso \u2014 should I worry that `LinuxAcademyIE()` instantiation triggers `_real_initialize`? No \u2014 `_real_initialize` is called from `initialize()` which is called by `extract()`, not `_real_extract()`. We call `_real_extract` directly \u2192 no login attempt. \u2713 Good (the IE has _real_initial